On the current API version, you should read and write assignees exclusively through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. The response will include an `assignees` array, such as:
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    { "login": "octocat" }
  ]
}
```

On the current API version, you should read the assigned users of an issue through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. The response will include an array of assignees, such as:
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    {
      "login": "octocat"
    }
  ]
}
```
This is according to the current-version.md documentation, which states: "The singular **`assignee` field has been removed.** Use the `assignees` array instead." and also mentions that when "Migrating from 2022-11-28:" you should "read and write assignees exclusively through the `assignees` array."

On the current API version, you should read the assigned users of an issue through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. The `assignees` array contains objects with a `login` field, such as `{ "login": "octocat" }`, as shown in the example: 
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    { "login": "octocat" }
  ]
}
```

According to the current-version.md document, you should use the `assignees` array to read the assigned users of an issue, as the singular `assignee` field has been removed. 

The response will include an `assignees` array, like this: 
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    {
      "login": "octocat"
    }
  ]
}
```

On the current API version, you should read and write assignees exclusively through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. The `assignees` array contains information about the assigned users, such as their `login` details. For example: 
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    { "login": "octocat" }
  ]
}
```

On the current API version, you should read the assigned users of an issue through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. The response will include a JSON object with an `assignees` array, such as: 
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    { "login": "octocat" }
  ]
}
```

On the current API version, you should read the assigned users of an issue through the `assignees` array instead of the old singular `assignee` field. According to the documentation, "The singular **`assignee` field has been removed.** Use the `assignees` array instead." 

For example, the response would include: 
```json
{
  "number": 10,
  "assignees": [
    { "login": "octocat" }
  ]
}
```

